"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including the preoperative PSA of 8.3 and the pathological findings demonstrating pT3b disease with extraprostatic extension. Significant adverse features include positive surgical margins, seminal vesicle invasion, lymphovascular invasion, and documented lymph node metastasis. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison shows a difference in Gleason patterns, with the initial biopsies reporting higher grades (Gleason 4+3 and 3+5) compared to the prostatectomy specimen's ISUP grade group 2.\n\nThe presence of positive margins, seminal vesicle invasion, and nodal metastasis are strong negative prognostic indicators. The documented lymph node sampling provides a definitive status rather than widening uncertainty due to missing nodes. Future prognosis assessment would be significantly revised by postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory following this initial evaluation. The fixed estimated horizon is 42.09 months. This is a predicted recurrence horizon, not evidence of observed recurrence. The sensitivity range is 33.67–50.51 months; its coverage has not been validated."
